/-!
# Regular sequences in a quotient of a flat local extension

Let `R → S` be a flat local homomorphism of Noetherian local rings, let `I ⊆ R` be an ideal
contained in the maximal ideal and let `J ⊆ S` be an ideal containing `IS`. This file proves one
of the two implications of [Stacks, Tag 09PX]: if `I` is generated by a regular sequence of `R`
and `J / IS` is generated by a regular sequence of `S / IS`, then `J` itself is generated by a
regular sequence of `S` and `S / J` has finite Tor dimension as a module over `R / I`.

Concatenating a regular sequence generating `IS` with lifts of a regular sequence generating
`J / IS` gives a regular sequence generating `J`; faithful flatness of a flat local map carries
the regular sequence generating `I` up to one generating `IS`. For the Tor statement, a finite
projective resolution of `S / J` over `S / IS` — which exists because that module has finite
projective dimension over `S / IS`, by the regular sequence — is flat over `R / I` because
`S / IS` is, so it is a K-flat representative and computes `Tor` in the required range.

## Main definitions

* `Ideal.IsGeneratedByRegularSequence`: an ideal is spanned by the entries of a regular list.
* `Stacks.CompleteIntersection.FiniteTorDimension`: all `Tor` groups of a module against every
  module vanish above one uniform degree.
* `Stacks.CompleteIntersection.quotientModule`: the target of a ring homomorphism, regarded as a
  module over its source.

## Main results

* `Stacks.CompleteIntersection.isGeneratedByRegularSequence_and_finiteTorDimension`: the
  implication described above.
* `Stacks.CompleteIntersection.isZero_tensorComplex_of_eventualTor`: eventual vanishing of the
  `Tor` groups of a module against a fixed second argument gives vanishing of the cohomology of
  the termwise tensor complex of any bounded above flat representative with that argument.
* `Stacks.CompleteIntersection.flat_quotient_map_of_flat`: `S / IS` is flat over `R / I` when `S`
  is flat over `R`.

## Implementation notes

The private `bridge*` declarations replay the K-flat-resolution calculation of
`CochainComplex.Tor.ChangeOfRingsSpectralSequence`, identifying the categorical `Tor` of the
statement with the homology of tensoring a flat representative with the second argument.

The regular closed fibre hypothesis of the source tag is not needed for this implication and is
therefore not assumed; it is needed for the converse, which is proved in
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.DividedPowers.CompleteIntersection.RegularSequenceTFAE` together with the
full equivalence.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09PX](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09PX)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Stacks.CompleteIntersection

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits
